    correctAnswers: ["C"],
    rationale: "A malformed call (missing a required parameter) is a protocol-level problem the client made, reported as a JSON-RPC error; a nonexistent user or an unavailable upstream service are both execution-time outcomes of a validly-formed call, so both belong in the tool result as isError: true, letting the model reason about them. Why not the others: A and D each collapse a distinction MCP's design intentionally separates — malformed requests are not the same category of failure as a validly-invoked tool that failed to produce a result; B misclassifies the nonexistent-user case, which is an execution outcome, not a protocol violation.",
    topicTag: "MCP Protocol vs Tool-Level Errors",
